  "suspect_nets": ["and_cross0", "and_cross1", "and_same0", "and_same1"],
  "algebraic_masking_correct": true,
  "vulnerability_summary": "The masked_and module is algebraically correct: q0 XOR q1 equals (a0 XOR a1) AND (b0 XOR b1) for every input combination in steady state, confirmed across all vectors in inputs/sim_trace.vcd.txt. The masking algebra itself is NOT flawed. The leakage instead arises from glitch propagation through unregistered combinational recombination logic. The module has no pipeline registers between the input ports (a0,a1,b0,b1,r) and the output ports (q0,q1): and_same0 (a0&b0), and_same1 (a1&b1), and_cross0 (a0&b1), and_cross1 (a1&b0) are all computed combinationally and merged in the same clock cycle at the q1 = and_same1 ^ and_cross0 ^ and_cross1 ^ r XOR stage (and q0 = and_same0 ^ r). Because a0, a1, b0, and b1 do not arrive at these gates with identical delay in real silicon (differing fan-in depths and wire/gate delays), and_cross0 and and_cross1 experience transient glitches during which signals derived from both shares of the same operand (a0 and a1, or b0 and b1) are simultaneously influencing switching activity on the same node before it settles. This transient joint dependency on both shares of an operand within a single clock cycle defeats the first-order independence (non-completeness) property that the masking scheme relies on, even though the final settled value of q0/q1 is correctly masked. An attacker with power/EM measurement capability can perform univariate CPA/DPA on the transient activity at and_cross0, and_cross1, or the q1 combination node to recover a first-order-exploitable correlation with the unmasked secret, despite the masking being algebraically sound at steady state.",
  "hardening_proposal": {
    "registers_to_add": ["a0", "a1", "b0", "b1", "r"],
    "placement_description": "Insert registers on a0, a1, b0, b1, and r at the module's input boundary, clocking them into flip-flops before they feed the and_same0, and_same1, and_cross0, and_cross1 partial-product gates and the subsequent q0/q1 XOR combination stage. Equivalently, registers could instead be placed on and_same0, and_same1, and_cross0, and_cross1, and r immediately after the partial-product AND gates but strictly before the final XOR tree that produces q0 and q1. Either placement ensures that within a single clock cycle no combinational path carries a glitch derived from both shares of the same operand (a0 and a1 together, or b0 and b1 together) into a shared node; each share pair is latched and stabilized before recombination, restoring cycle-by-cycle non-completeness/glitch-robustness. Registering only the final q0/q1 outputs would be insufficient, since the glitch-vulnerable recombination at and_cross0/and_cross1/the q1 XOR node would still occur upstream of such a register within the same cycle."
  },
  "confidence": 0.95
}
